WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.180 --> 00:00:04.740
Modern science generates a lot of data —

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:09.380
amounts far beyond the ability
of humans to process on their own.

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:13.300
This is why scientists often
rely on computational methods

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:19.620
such as machine learning — a type of
artificial intelligence to crunch big data.

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:24.140
In fact, it's pretty commonplace today,
and is even used in applications like

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:28.920
Spotify and Netflix to help
predict your media preferences.

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:30.960
But there's a problem.

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:34.180
Traditional approaches to machine
learning, such as deep learning,

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:38.440
are focused on classification
and statistical correlation.

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:42.000
This makes them very useful for
recognising and comparing patterns

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.000
in things like audio or images,
but not very good at discovering

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:49.480
what might have generated
the patterns in the first place.

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:52.060
Understanding these so-called
generating mechanisms —

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:54.800
in other words the dynamics
of cause and effect —

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:58.480
is a fundamental challenge
across all scientific fields,

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:02.820
from decoding how a complex biological
cell arises from DNA, to figuring out

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:07.540
how a thunderstorm forms from differences
in air temperature and humidity.

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:11.160
Such tasks require abilities
such as inference or reasoning,

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:14.980
which most artificial
intelligences are not designed for.

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:18.320
To tackle these challenges, one
team of scientists are developing

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:21.720
a revolutionary new form
of machine learning,

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:26.660
which they've called causal deconvolution
by algorithmic generative models,

00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:30.940
which basically means breaking down complicated and interconnected datasets

00:01:30.940 --> 00:01:34.660
to look at the underlying mechanisms
or code that generate them.

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:39.760
It aims to find the individual underlying
algorithms that give rise to a dataset,

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:45.660
in a sense, treating natural phenomena as
computer programmes generating observed data.

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:49.480
So, we can think about this with a
visual analogy — if we have a table

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:53.340
with several objects on it and we lay
a cloth over it, we're presented with

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:57.880
a unique 3D terrain and this
represents a mass of collected data.

00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:01.860
Some of it relates to the objects
underneath and some doesn't.

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:05.380
Modern machine learning — which
is based on classical statistics —

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:08.349
is good at identifying objects
by comparing features

00:02:08.349 --> 00:02:11.800
such as shape to previous
examples it's been shown.

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:16.920
But what it can't do is tell us much about
how the objects underneath were formed.

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:20.960
The aim of this new methodology is to
first work out which bits of the terrain

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:25.020
relate to the different objects — 
this is the deconvolution part —

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:29.520
and then to identify the objects by
looking at how they might be generated.

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:34.620
This is done by testing lots of possible coffee cup,
hat or book generating algorithms,

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:37.960
and looking at how these could
influence the tablecloth arrangement.

00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:41.700
So unlike traditional machine learning,
it attempts to infer how the objects

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:45.180
might have been generated
to match the observed data.

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:48.860
But this isn't just intended to
work on imaginary dinosaurs.

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:52.240
It's hoped that it can be applied to
help scientists unravel the dynamics

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:56.880
of cause and effect in fields such
as genetics and cell biology —

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:01.860
for example, in understanding the complicated 
gene interactions that give rise to cancer —

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:05.601
and that's because this methodology
moves away from classification

00:03:05.601 --> 00:03:10.020
and towards questions of causation,
providing insights and models

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:15.060
to help us understand the underlying
mechanisms behind observed data.

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:19.360
So, as machine intelligence continues
to develop, researchers may become

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:24.880
increasingly reliant on AIs which think less
like machines and more like scientists.

